# Extracting Text from a Document in JavaScript

The approach depends heavily on what kind of "document" you mean. Here are the most common cases with working implementations.

## 1. Plain text / HTML text file (Browser)

```javascript
/**
 * Reads a File/Blob and returns its text content.
 */
async function extractTextFromFile(file) {
  return await file.text();
}

// Usage with an <input type="file">
document.querySelector('#fileInput').addEventListener('change', async (e) => {
  const text = await extractTextFromFile(e.target.files[0]);
  console.log(text);
});
```

`Blob.text()` is supported in all modern browsers and handles UTF-8 decoding for you.

## 2. HTML document → visible text (Browser)

If you have an HTML string or a DOM node and want only the readable text:

```javascript
function extractTextFromHtml(html) {
  const doc = new DOMParser().parseFromString(html, 'text/html');
  return doc.body.innerText || doc.body.textContent;
}

// From the live page:
const pageText = document.body.innerText;
```

`innerText` respects CSS visibility and inserts line breaks; `textContent` grabs everything including hidden nodes.

## 3. PDF (Browser, using pdf.js)

PDFs require a parser — text isn't stored as plain strings.

```html
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/pdf.js/3.11.174/pdf.min.js"></script>
```

```javascript
async function extractTextFromPdf(file) {
  const pdfjsLib = window['pdfjs-dist/build/pdf'] || window.pdfjsLib;
  pdfjsLib.GlobalWorkerOptions.workerSrc =
    'https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/pdf.js/3.11.174/pdf.worker.min.js';

  const data = await file.arrayBuffer();
  const pdf = await pdfjsLib.getDocument({ data }).promise;

  let fullText = '';
  for (let i = 1; i <= pdf.numPages; i++) {
    const page = await pdf.getPage(i);
    const content = await page.getTextContent();
    // Join items, preserving line breaks based on transform Y position
    let lastY = null;
    let pageText = '';
    for (const item of content.items) {
      const y = item.transform[5];
      if (lastY !== null && Math.abs(y - lastY) > 1) pageText += '\n';
      pageText += item.str;
      lastY = y;
    }
    fullText += pageText + '\n\n';
  }
  return fullText.trim();
}
```

## 4. DOCX (Browser, using mammoth.js)

```html
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/mammoth/1.6.0/mammoth.browser.min.js"></script>
```

```javascript
async function extractTextFromDocx(file) {
  const arrayBuffer = await file.arrayBuffer();
  const result = await mammoth.extractRawText({ arrayBuffer });
  return result.value;
}
```

## 5. In Node.js (file on disk)

```javascript
const fs = require('fs/promises');

async function extractTextFromPath(path) {
  return await fs.readFile(path, 'utf8');
}
```

For PDFs in Node, use `pdf-parse` or `pdfjs-dist`; for DOCX, use `mammoth` (Node build).

## Choosing the right function

| Source | Function | Needs a library? |
|--------|----------|------------------|
| .txt / .html file | `file.text()` | No |
| HTML string/DOM | `DOMParser` / `.innerText` | No |
| PDF | `pdf.js` | Yes |
| DOCX | `mammoth.js` | Yes |
| File on disk (Node) | `fs.readFile` | No |

## Notes & caveats

- **Encoding**: `file.text()` assumes UTF-8. For other encodings, decode manually with `TextDecoder`:
  ```javascript
  const buf = await file.arrayBuffer();
  const text = new TextDecoder('windows-1252').decode(buf);
  ```
- **Scanned PDFs** contain images, not text — you'd need OCR (e.g., Tesseract.js).
- **`innerText` vs `textContent`**: `innerText` triggers layout and is slower but mirrors what a user sees; `textContent` is faster and includes hidden text.
- **Line breaks in PDFs**: pdf.js gives positioned text fragments with no inherent newlines, which is why the Y-position heuristic above is needed.

Tell me which document type and runtime (browser vs Node) you're targeting, and I can trim this down to a single focused function.

